ification of  clonotypes but also a lot of  kind of results that are difficult to  deal with for example in this project  the end result was  a combination of  kind of novel sequences that no one  really  knew what to do with and so all of a  sudden a lot of questions appeared what  do you do with all of these sequences  which ones are the more important and  significant for the treatment that was  being studied and then also how can we  uh kind of create some kind of a  large-scale uh process so that we don't  have to deal with this in a manual way  going through blast and kind of  annotating results manually  so what we were able to do here is to  use statistical analysis to identify  clusters of clonal types that were  really  more abundant  in the  specific types of treatments that were  being researched here  and then  use some standard tools to predict  epitopes with good binding  properties and then use a distance  matrix or a dissimilarity matrix between  the sequences of these predicted  epitopes to then start asking questions  about what are some of the expected and  unexpected epitopes that we're finding  to really try to annotate also a lot of  the unknown sequences that were  identified  and so this project took a longer time  but i think it's a good example when you  know someone has uh maybe some specific  questions in mind and and it's typically  a bioinformatics  you know kind of a challenge to get  stuck at a certain stage and then try to  identify some yeah i think creative ways  to get out of that um situation  finally another example is an example  with single cell sequencing where uh i  think 10x genomics is a new uh sequence  a new capability that  the center has now  and  we were dealing with a project where  different time points for different cell  types where really  you know a lot of  information  is  there but we were tasked with  just kind of annotating the different  types of cells and then using a standard  procedure more or less toda