ing paths  to learning and mastering bioinformatics  and what that means is that you can  appreciate bioinformatics but maybe not  have enough time to master all of the  different requirements for the technical  uh kind of you know  all the way to statistical and machine  learning and so there's really a lot to  learn so our focus is to making it  available and lowering the barrier of  entry and we do that by packaging these  complex technical things into short  coursework that is available online  and can be taken asynchronously and so  from this pilot program  we were surprised to see that a lot of  the participants  a lot of them were from hawaii  from unmc in nebraska from other places  were able to go through that whole  process and we had these five options  that people could choose from  introduction to bioinformatics which is  a very basic program introductory  program for someone who doesn't know  anything about it data science which is  focused on machine learning statistical  analysis coding and r and python  genomics which is focused on variant  analysis for cancer and infectious  diseases  metagenomics and bioinformatics for  infectious diseases primarily because  there's such an interest in covet 19.  and so what that means and i'll just  give you two examples here  in our introduction to bioinformatics  program which is by the way available  for free across nepi and lbrn  is a  brief introduction to the applications  of bioinformatics the need of  bioinformatics in different domains  the intersection between biological and  data concepts and an introduction to the  technical side of bioinformatics which  is coding in bio in r and python  in addition to that we have  specialization tracks that are really  papers  from that domain  explained in terms of their  bioinformatics methodology providing  with the technical and reproducible  tools for someone else to replicate that  study and that it covers oncology  infectious diseases neuroscience and  agriculture  now many of these examples are like i  said available through coding and  non-coding tracks  and that means that we have a variety of  tools that one can use to produce plots  that are standard and used  and learn along the way while building  pipelines  what those pipelines represent and how  do they transform data into something  that is biologically interpretable  in addition to that for those that  decide to go deeper and beyond that we  also offer a full process from context  to the logic of the analysis to then  practical tools that they can  kind of learn themselves using  different tools like google colab  to not only do the analysis but then  also save that code that they could  potentially replicate in the future and  so like i said one of the major  objectives is to elevate the level of  biomedical research by bringing a lot  more students into the bioinformatics  space and giving them the tools to get  started quickly because i think most of  the learning actually happen  when you do the learning when you do the  analysis and so as you struggle to come  up with the results and you have to find  out how to do a certain figure you know  that's really when you learn so that's  really the concept of that  and uh to support that the online portal  that we have designed  called omixlogic and i'm just going to  put the link here in the main chat if  anyone wants to take a look at that it  is  learn.omixlogic.com the sign up is free  um anyone can get started there are a  lot of free resources available  moreover it's very easy to go from the  very basic and free to the much more  advanced and kind of research oriented  type of type of types of activity  and those include not only activities  for learning but also activities to  understand  how students learn and that is another  important topic i think in the domain of  bioinformatics and data science  education is that the approach is to  give tools and and kind of show skills  but in reality the learning happens um  with a lot of variation across different  types of learning styles and learning  techniques and so we're always excited  to incorporate new ideas that come from  experts that are already a part of this  exciting network and so just as an  example of that we've been working with  lbrn to identify important resources  that are already being taught and used  throughout the network to incorporate  them into our learning portal and make  them available to students across the  network  one of these  is by dr jimanchi raje  from nicholls state university a  graduate of lsu who developed a course  that is important for students who are  joining any kind of biological  curricula  to incorporate the data component into  that as well and so he goes from the  very basics of understanding what does  biological data look like to a little  bit of coding and understanding how to  use packages in python so that one is  able to appreciate  the need and the application of  bioinformatics in life sciences in  general  so to support that process we're  actually making all of these resources  available  for those who are interested to  contribute coursework and and make that  coursework available throughout the  network and that means that we can  support development of training  materials  develop easy to use data analysis tools  help analyze their data set and and turn  publications into a tutorial  and also help develop interactive data  stories around those data sets and  important research topics so that  students can really benefit  from that  now i'll finish with my last slide where  i'm gonna just mention some upcoming  opportunities  so recently we started this discussion  about this upcoming summer  what is really important in the summer  research program how can students get  some training and  expertise in bioinformatics  for the previous two summers i think we  had a lot of interest  between 50 and 100 students each summer  we're participating in the research  training  on different topics and we often have a  lot of different topics at the same time  so i think on one hand that's a benefit  a lot of students can pursue what  they're already interested in in another  way that's a limitation because people  don't understand what goes into that  training and so what we're going to do  this summer is we're going to talk about  single cell and bulk rna seq  transcriptomics and cancer  we're going to talk about covid19 again  and continue uh offering training on  variants of concern as well as  microbiome and metagenomics in long  covet  we'll have a training program for  machine learning and data visualization  in bioinformatics using python and  plotly and building interactive  dashboards to make exploration of data  easier  and we hope to introduce a new  specialization which is going to be  focused on agrogenomics  and we'll use the example of cannabis  to talk about genomics and  transcriptomics for agriculture and  biotechnology  so with that again thank you all for  listening to my presentation i'm happy  to answer any questions that you might  have and if you're interested you can  also get in touch with me directly my  email is amelia pine.bio  thank you everyone  all right if anybody has any questions  for chris or elia  the floor is up  that was very informative by the way  chris is the  ceo of pine bio i  i missed in introducing him and  helio  that curves  yeah it's my apologies once again  alia is the ceo of  pine biotech and  [Music]  he mentioned the tauber institute and i  think  they've had  located at university of haifa  as the bioinformatics part of it was led  by his dad dr leonidroski  and  he's been a tremendous  asset for us and  many of our  researchers in the lbrn network have  have availed  the services  of pine biotech through the lbrn core  through the gene lab core and  i'd like  i'd like to believe that so far they've  been happy and i've heard gotten  feedback from a bunch of them saying  they are  very happy with the way interactions  have taken place  anybody has any questions for  dr christopher taylor or mr elia brotzky  okay  all right um gus wants  um well take uh now is the time for a  brief pause we are actually five seven  minutes into our pause so we'll  reconvene in  um  seven minutes time and for our next  session  and gus will introduce our next speaker  then
 

